One option is we could mark
the alternative text just a
World Wildlife Fund logo.

222
00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:33.610
When we think about
the experience

223
00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:36.640
of someone who is using
an assistive technology,
they might hear

224
00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:40.090
out bullet number five, "Donate
to the World Wildlife Fund,"

225
00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:42.930
and the next thing you hear
is "World Wildlife Fund logo."

226
00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.640
Maybe that's just not the most
useful piece of information here.

227
00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:51.020
So that's the other option
in the recommended actions
drop down I covered.

228
00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:54.550
Mark as decorative,
so let's do that, and that

229
00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:58.770
will just basically treat
it as an invisible object.

230
00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:00.810
It's not conveying content -

231
00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:07.680
just to avoid giving
someone too much redundant
information, so let's mark

232
00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:08.680
it as decorative.

233
00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:14.960
And, great, now our images and
all text are gone from this list.

234
00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:20.400
While we're on the subject
of images, there's one more
thing that's calling out here.

235
00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:23.600
Images or objects not
inline.

236
00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:26.070
Let's take a look at that and
see what that's pointing to.

237
00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:31.200
That takes us back to
our first image, and in

238
00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:36.250
the Accessibility checker,
again, it gives us kind of this
Why Fix? and Steps to Fix.

239
00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:39.570
It's saying that if
the image or object

240
00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:42.180
is not inline, it may be
difficult for a screen reader

241
00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:44.900
or assisted technology users
to find it in your document

242
00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:51.320
and can make it a little
harder to interact with.

243
00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:55.310
Usually, the easiest fix
here is to always use the
inline text wrapping.

244
00:16:56.460 --> 00:17:00.790
The image I just opened the
Layout options, I can also go

245
00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:05.370
to the Picture Format tab
of the ribbon and choose my
text wrapping options there.

246
00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.790
Let's mark it as
In Line with text.

247
00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:12.990
That's one more error down.

248
00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:20.090
While we're still talking about
that, I do want to mention
another tidbit of information.

249
00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:26.250
While in line is the generally
recommended and better option

250
00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.070
for accessibility, there may

251
00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:32.360
be scenarios where another
text wrapping option does
suit your document better.

252
00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:36.100
Maybe if there's columns
or things like that.

253
00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:42.150
If that's the case, some
of these other text
wrapping options can work

254
00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:45.280
as long as there's a couple
other steps you take.

255
00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:48.550
So let's do square text
wrapping, for example.

256
00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:54.900
Some of these other text
wrapping options just give us

257
00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:57.330
a little bit more flexibility
in how that image is placed.

258
00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:02.970
So we have that error
again about the image or
object not being inline.

259
00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:06.240
You may notice that
there's an anchor icon

260
00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:09.470
in the left margin of my
document alongside the image.

261
00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:13.079
That's a solution
that can improve the

262
00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:16.290
accessibility of these
other text wrapping options.

263
00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:21.040
The anchor basically indicates
where in your document the
image should be read,

264
00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:24.255
and that way it does
get discovered by

265
00:18:24.256 --> 00:18:27.390
assistive technology so
the user would find it.

266
00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:31.630
And I can basically click and drag
that anchor to wherever I want it.

267
00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:38.810
So maybe I wanted this image
to be talking about why the
Amazon Rainforest matters,

268
00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:42.190
so I can drag it down now,

269
00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:46.690
and now that means the
image will be read out to

270
00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:50.450
a user right around the
same time they are reading
all that information.

271
00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:56.600
That said, the in-line text
wrapping is the best option.

272
00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:58.790
So we'd recommend that
wherever possible.

273
00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:03.816
So one more down.

274
00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:06.600
We have one more error
and one more warning here.

275
00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:10.160
Our error looks like it's in
the table that's up above.

276
00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:15.430
I have included this
table that is basically

277
00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:20.020
a percentage of the Amazon
Rainforest contained in
South American countries.

278
00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:28.060
Brazil has almost 60%;
Columbia about 7%; Argentina
and Chile have 0%.

279
00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:30.620
So they don't have any
of the Amazon Rainforest
in their borders.

280
00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:38.480
This error is calling out that my
table doesn't have a header row.

281
00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:39.780
It looks like it does.

282
00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:43.010
I have my country header
and my percentage of
Amazon Rainforest header.

283
00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:45.620
But it's not quite there.

284
00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:50.270
This error is referring to the
kind of semantic structure of it.

285
00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:55.910
And so in assistive technology,
basically, looks at the markup

286
00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:59.760
or the semantic structure
of the table to convey
certain relationships,

287
00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.210
such that if a user is
moving between columns,

288
00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:06.510
they would get the appropriate
row or column header read out.

289
00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:10.700
It's always good to add
a header row to a table.

290
00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:13.790
Let's go ahead and do that.

291
00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:15.410
I will select my table.

292
00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:20.600
I can either mark it as a
lay out table that's
intended to lay out content.

293
00:20:22.071 --> 00:20:24.920
I think that's fairly
a new feature actually.

294
00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:28.440
The main option is Use
first row as header.

295
00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:32.190
In a down [phonetic]
table, that's usually the
most common approach that

296
00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:34.320
the column headers are
at the very first row,

297
00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:40.120
and so we can just make
that change in line.

298
00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:44.970
There's no visual change to
that, but it does make the mark

299
00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:49.360
up just a little more clear
for someone using assistive
technology to read through it.

300
00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:58.320
I can also do that manually
in the table design tab of

301
00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:01.480
the ribbon when I have the
table selected, I'd be able

302
00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:05.640
to select that first row and
check Header Row here and any

303
00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:10.310
other information I'd like
to convey in that table, I
can check appropriately.

304
00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:14.640
The critical one's just
making sure you have
that header row marked.

305
00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.860
Now we have a warning
here about merged or
split cells in the table.

306
00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:33.360
I made a couple edits to
this table when I was
building it that intended

307
00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:37.000
to make it a little bit
easier to consume, but

308
00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.780
it does make the table
structure slightly more complex.

309
00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:46.400
And why that's problematic
is when someone's using
assistive technology,

310
00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:48.670
they would be navigating up

311
00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:51.260
or down in the column and
then bouncing between columns.

312
00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:59.210
So let's say I've gone through
the first five rows of the
table and the format is, okay,

313
00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:05.570
I get my country, and then to
the right of it, I get the
percentage, country, percentage;

314
00:22:08.420 --> 00:22:11.670
I get down here,
I'm on Guyana, and then

315
00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.370
I move over to the next cell
and then I get French Guyana.

316
00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:18.500
That's a little confusing
because someone was expecting
to get a percentage there

317
00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:20.720
and might be wondering why that
structure's a little bit funky.

318
00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:26.070
Let's go ahead
and change that.

319
00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:31.230
I intentionally grouped
these on the same line, but
let's separate them out.

320
00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:48.580
So I'm just getting rid of the
-- fixing [phonetic] those.

321
00:22:51.140 --> 00:23:12.220
I've added my second row;
I'm going to use the Merge
column feature, great.

322
00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:13.130
As I worked through
that, you may

323
00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:17.500
have noticed that in the
accessibility checker those
errors slowly went away.

324
00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:27.180
So great, so that's one more
error down, and then it's
telling us that this cell has 0%

325
00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:31.830
in it, is another merged
cell which can impact

326
00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:33.620
the experience for
someone with a disability.

327
00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:40.380
So they recommend just
having unique lines for
each row in the table.

328
00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:48.920
Let's go ahead and split that
cell, let's turn it into two rows.

329
00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:56.350
Now I've added my 0% in
Argentina as well as Chile now.

330
00:23:58.560 --> 00:23:59.110
Cool.

331
00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:00.860
And you may now notice back

332
00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:04.490
in the accessibility
checker, it says no
accessibility issues found.

333
00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:07.540
People shouldn't have issues
reading this document.

334
00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:12.000
While we're on the subject,

335
00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:17.890
I wanted to briefly touch on
another best practice, another
couple best practices in here.

336
00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:22.860
Back to my agenda slide,
there's one on heading structure.

337
00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:28.180
I did already cover
conveying information by
color alone, but let's take

338
00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:29.190
a look at hyperlinks,

339
00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.300
and then we'll do a couple
things in PowerPoint.

340
00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:38.520
So in this document,
I have some links here.

341
00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:42.480
I have this one that says
"click here," and under

342
00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:45.530
my Deforestation heading,
I have another link that
says "click here."

343
00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:51.690
Using assistive technology,
the user may get confused by that.

344
00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:58.270
The way a link would be read
like that is it would just
read, "link, click here."

345
00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:03.520
And so it would read "link, click
here" and then "link, click here."

346
00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:13.520
So it's difficult for a
user to differentiate what
those links are pointing to.

347
00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:15.980
Let's give them a little
bit more descriptive name.

348
00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:23.890
I think my hyperlink went to
Wikipedia, let me just copy that.

349
00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:28.540
Let me craft a new sentence
and we can try it out, and
I can get rid of that one.

350
00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:45.040
So now I made a new sentence
that reads "Check out

351
00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:48.810
the link Wikipedia article on the
Amazon Rainforest to learn more."

352
00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:51.150
So I said the word
"link" in there just

353
00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:53.270
to indicate how an assistive
technology would read it.

354
00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:59.190
So when we use that kind
of descriptive link,
that's very clear.

355
00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:04.040
A user knows right away where
it's supposed to take them
and doesn't have to guess.

356
00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:06.360
So let's go ahead and remove
that previous sentence.

357
00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:08.520
That's better.

358
00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:16.130
Let me again cut the
hyperlink, and let's do
the same thing down here -

359
00:26:16.130 --> 00:26:18.180
this is my section
on deforestation.

360
00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:45.170
Now I added, "Visit this
page for link more
statistics on deforestation.

361
00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:49.860
Not only are the links
themselves more descriptive

362
00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:51.360
and the user knows where
they are going to go.

363
00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:56.840
If someone's navigating
link to link, they now
know which is which.

364
00:26:57.410 --> 00:27:01.910
So that's the other
important factor there.

365
00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:04.430
So next I mentioned I was
going to cover headings.

366
00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:09.260
And so, in this document,
it's broken out into sections.

367
00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:13.550
So the very beginning, I
have a document title "The
Amazon Rainforest," I have

368
00:27:13.550 --> 00:27:19.080
a heading for "Deforestation,"
"Why it Matters," and "So,
what can you do?"

369
00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:25.260
But for those of you who
are Word power users,

370
00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:30.390
you may know that when you
add true heading styles from

371
00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:35.610
the style section of the
ribbon, they can look

372
00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:38.280
a certain way, but they also get
added to the Navigation pane.

373
00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:43.110
So you may notice that I have
one item in the navigation
pane, a Bibliography.

374
00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:50.790
When I select that in styles
tab of the ribbon, it's
using a Heading 1 style.

375
00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:53.640
Headings are really
important for conveying the
structure of your document.

376
00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:59.900
Let's go ahead and add some
headings to the document -

377
00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:04.520
add some heading styles,
because I already have where
my section headers are.

378
00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:09.300
So with this first one,
"The Amazon Rainforest," we select

379
00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:12.920
that, I'll mark that
as a heading level 1.

380
00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:16.850
It now shows up in the
navigation pane, it adds

381
00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:20.800
some other heading
features, such as being
able to expand or collapse.

382
00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:27.880
And then so the way
headings work is -

383
00:28:30.910 --> 00:28:38.320
Heading 1 indicates the start
of the content or kind of the
main area of my content.

384
00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:41.380
Heading 1 is always kind
about the main body.

385
00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:45.130
Within that main body,
I have section headings.

386
00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:51.480
So within the body, I would
make all of my section
headings a Heading 2.

387
00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:55.490
So "Deforestation" and
Why it Matters" would both

388
00:28:55.490 --> 00:29:00.190
be Heading 2 because they're
subsections of the Heading 1.

389
00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:03.790
In this "Why it
Matters" section -

390
00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:07.550
or sorry - I have one more
here, "So, what can you do?"

391
00:29:10.190 --> 00:29:12.320
We also make that a Heading 2.

392
00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:14.670
As I do that, these get
added to the navigation pane.

393
00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:19.540
I have one more here --
"Here are some ideas."

394
00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:28.350
So in the Heading 2 of "So,
what you can do," there's a
section of some specific ideas.

395
00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:33.130
So I would say that's a subsection
of "So, what can you do?"

396
00:29:33.830 --> 00:29:37.620
Let me mark that
as a Heading 3.

397
00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:40.850
You kind of see my
heading structure get
built out a little bit.

398
00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:45.786
For someone who is using
assistive technology,

399
00:29:45.787 --> 00:29:47.720
they are able to navigate
just by headings.

400
00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:50.810
So using a clean heading
structure like this, that

401
00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:53.820
is indicating the layout
of the content, gives them

402
00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:57.400
some idea of how best
they can most efficiently
read through my content.

403
00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:06.050
And it's always a good practice
to only have one Heading 1.

404
00:30:06.050 --> 00:30:07.310
There's a couple
exceptions to that.

405
00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:10.510
In this document, for example,
that makes the most sense.

406
00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:14.950
So let's select our Bibliography
and mark it a Heading 2.

407
00:30:14.950 --> 00:30:19.160
So now everything in the document
is a subsection of the main body.

408
00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:23.790
I can expand or
collapse these.

409
00:30:23.790 --> 00:30:27.740
So they're a really cool
way to clean up my -- if I
have a really long document.

410
00:30:29.080 --> 00:30:32.390
You may notice that the
headings weren't indicated in
the accessibility checker.

411
00:30:34.810 --> 00:30:38.440
Today, I think the only error the
accessibility checker flags are -

412
00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:42.890
I think it's a tip that's just
kind of a recommendation, is

413
00:30:42.890 --> 00:30:49.570
if you have a really long
document, like over ten pages,
I'm not sure what the exact rule

414
00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:52.500
of thumb is, but when you have
really long documents, it's going

415
00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:57.570
to recommend that you add
headings to make it easier
to consume that document.

416
00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:03.470
That's one example of
something that we didn't
cover but would show

417
00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:05.660
up in the accessibility
checker in some scenarios.

418
00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:09.560
Back to my status bar
in the lower left, we

419
00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:12.210
now have this icon that says
Accessibility Good to go.

420
00:31:13.130 --> 00:31:14.630
Now that we've done
a lot of these -

421
00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:19.030
fixed the accessibility
checker errors and warnings
and tips and done some

422
00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:20.660
of these other best practices,

423
00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:23.600
it's telling us that our
document should be a good
experience for users.

424
00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:27.410
So great, we made some good
progress on that Word document.

425
00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:31.190
Let's switch back
over to PowerPoint.

426
00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:35.670
And so I have this PowerPoint
open so let me close out of it.

427
00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:38.730
There's a couple things
I want to call out here.

428
00:31:41.950 --> 00:31:47.150
A lot of the things that I
covered in Word pretty much
apply one to one to Outlook.

429
00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:51.410
There's some
exceptions to that.

430
00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:57.200
There's some nuances to the
format the PowerPoint is that
makes it a little bit different.

431
00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:06.500
As opposed to how the sequence
in which someone reads a
document, it is very linear.

432
00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:13.790
The document is read top to
bottom, someone can navigate
section by section, where

433
00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:14.730
at the core of
it, you don't have

434
00:32:14.730 --> 00:32:17.610
to do anything special
to control the reading
order other than just put

435
00:32:17.610 --> 00:32:19.120
the content in the
