&gt;&gt; Sure.

700
00:51:55.530 --> 00:52:01.200
&gt;&gt; This is from Jane,
and the question is: Do
you have any guidelines

701
00:52:01.690 --> 00:52:05.190
on when to add alt
description for

702
00:52:05.190 --> 00:52:09.520
an image changing to descriptive
decorator and for decorative.

703
00:52:09.980 --> 00:52:12.410
Would you then exclude
these from the slide order?

704
00:52:14.834 --> 00:52:17.366
&gt;&gt; That's a good question
on PowerPoint too.

705
00:52:17.990 --> 00:52:24.590
With imagens, my general rule
is they're conveying content
and they are meaningful

706
00:52:24.590 --> 00:52:28.240
and someone would gain information
from that image, you want

707
00:52:28.240 --> 00:52:30.460
to add good alt
text to describe it.

708
00:52:31.660 --> 00:52:35.510
If the image is not adding
a lot of content, such as

709
00:52:35.510 --> 00:52:39.540
on this last slide, my
image of the woman with the
question marks above her head.

710
00:52:41.380 --> 00:52:44.560
That one has alt text, but
that would actually be okay
to mark as decorative.

711
00:52:47.590 --> 00:52:59.310
And then in my reading order
pane, let me bring that back up.

712
00:52:59.660 --> 00:53:01.210
When I mark something
as decorative,

713
00:53:01.650 --> 00:53:04.540
it automatically gets deselected
from the reading order.

714
00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:09.290
From the reading order pane,
I can also mark stuff as
decorative really easily

715
00:53:09.290 --> 00:53:10.560
if I just check or uncheck it.

716
00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:14.530
So now it's not decorative
again, and you'd want

717
00:53:14.530 --> 00:53:16.110
to make sure it has
alt text in that case.

718
00:53:16.670 --> 00:53:18.080
And then if I uncheck
it, it marks it

719
00:53:18.080 --> 00:53:21.710
as decorative and then hides
it from the reading order.

720
00:53:22.060 --> 00:53:23.060
Good question.

721
00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:24.330
&gt;&gt; Okay, cool.

722
00:53:24.828 --> 00:53:26.710
